super().__init__() sets x = 1 from A, then B adds y = 2. The __dict__ holds both: {'x': 1, 'y': 2}.

code:
class A:
    def __init__(self):
        self.x = 1

class B(A):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.y = 2

print(B().__dict__)  # {'x': 1, 'y': 2}

